  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1258 PM MDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nSTRONG TO SEVERE STORMS POSSIBLE TODAY:\n\nStorms are already ongoing this morning across southeast Utah\nwith storms beginning to fire over the western Colorado high\nterrain with daytime heating and spreading into some valleys. We\nremain plenty unstable and moist as evident in our 18Z GJT\nsounding with surface CAPE of around 1400 J/kg and a forecast\nsurface CAPE around 2000 J/kg. Plenty of shear out there as well\nwith 30 kts so enough to sustain updrafts. Like yesterday, we\ncould see strong to potentially severe storms, capable of large\nhail and damaging winds in addition to heavy rain and frequent\nlightning. Hi res CAMs seem to indicate storm coverage and\nintensity ramping up late this afternoon into the evening hours.\nWe remain in the favored right entrance region of the upper level\njet giving us that dynamic forcing needed to support robust \nconvection.\n\nFLOOD WATCH WEDNESDAY AND THURSDAY:\n\nA strong low pressure trough remains to our west with high\npressure across the southeastern CONUS, keeping our CWA in the\ndirect moisture feed from the south that stretches from the Baja\nacross Arizona and New Mexico through the Four Corners and up\ninto eastern Utah and western Colorado. PWAT anomalies will\nremain around 200 percent of normal through the end of the week\nwith a surge in moisture Wednesday evening through Thursday\nafternoon where PWAT anomalies surge close to almost 300 percent\nof normal, mainly across southwest Colorado. Areas around SW\nColorado have received 1 to 2 inches of rainfall based on\nreports over the last 24 hours so this area remains saturated.\nStorm coverage will become more widespread Wednesday and\nThursday due to some robust shortwaves moving through Wednesday\nafternoon as well as another Wednesday night into Thursday.\nGiven the surge in deeper moisture, already saturated ground\nfrom recent rains, and burn scars in the region...decided to\nissue a Flood Watch for portions of SW Colorado including the SW\nCO valleys and San Juan mountains from Wednesday afternoon\nthrough Thursday night. Also, issued a Flood Watch for portions\nof SE Utah from midnight tonight through 6 am Thursday. This\nincludes the Babylon, Ferris, Gold Mountain and Elk burn scars\nin particular among other susceptible flood prone locales. Heavy\nrain is a near certainty with any storm that develops so other\nareas outside the Flood Watch could see impacts, but the Flood\nWatch is for where the best potential and highest confidence\nlies. Cannot rule out strong to severe storms as well given the\ndynamic forcing of the jet, instability and shear...but this may\nall depend on the amount of cloud cover as deeper moisture moves\nin Wednesday evening through Thursday night. \n\nFRIDAY AND BEYOND:\n\nFriday onward, forecast confidence decreases. Models are in \npoor agreement as to what happens once the West Coast trough \nejects to the northeast, with some solutions building a ridge, \nand others developing a Rex Block over the Southwest. Ensembles \ndo indicate a gradual drying trend, but keep PWATs of 100-150% \nof normal around through the weekend. That's enough to keep a \nchance of isolated to scattered terrain based showers and \nstorms in the forecast. However as we've seen with this type of\npattern in the past, models tend to be too quick to exit this\nmoisture and we could see stronger storms possible along that\nmoisture gradient as dry air tries to move in from the west. \nWith abundant cloud cover and shower activity expected through \nthe end of the week, temperatures will run near to around 5 \ndegrees below normal.",
